description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/memory.rs:403-408 and memory.rs:448-453

Both query_objects() and query_changes() build State values like:

    let query_state = State::from(
        inner
            .current_state(O::TYPE_NAME, account_id.as_ref())
            .to_string()
            .as_str(),
    );

State (in jmap-types) implements both From<String> and From<&str> via impl_string_newtype!. Calling .to_string().as_str() and then From<&str> performs an extra deref + reconversion that the From<String> impl would skip. Idiomatic Rust:

    let query_state = State::from(
        inner
            .current_state(O::TYPE_NAME, account_id.as_ref())
            .to_string(),
    );

The same call site shape is correct at memory.rs:316 (get_state) and memory.rs:378 (get_changes): they pass State::from(n.to_string()) without the .as_str() hop. The inconsistency between line 316 / 378 and lines 405 / 451 is a local style divergence that should be unified to the simpler form.

What goes wrong for callers when this stays as-is: nothing functional. This is pure idiom: the .as_str() reads as if the From<&str> impl is preferred, but From<String> exists and is more direct. A reader reasonably wonders 'why does this site convert through &str while the sibling site does not?' — small but real cognitive cost.

Picked up by clippy::useless_asref in some configurations; not by default. Mirror pattern likely exists in the 6 sibling MemoryBackends.
